<h2>The statutory duty to inform: Section 5</h2>
<p>Section 5 of the Act is the hinge on which the reporting stage turns, and it is routinely overlooked. Whenever a police officer, Protection Officer, service provider or Magistrate receives a complaint of domestic violence or is otherwise present at the place of an incident, each is obliged to inform the aggrieved woman of five things: her right to apply for the reliefs the Act provides — protection orders, monetary relief, custody orders, residence orders and compensation; the availability of the services of registered service providers; the availability of the services of Protection Officers; her right to free legal services under the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987; and her right to file a complaint under the cruelty provision of the penal law — Section 498A of the Indian Penal Code, now Section 85 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita — where the facts support it.</p>
<p>The design is deliberate. Parliament knew that the first official a frightened woman meets effectively controls what she learns about her options, and it refused to leave that to chance. The proviso adds the other half of the balance: nothing in the Act relieves the police of their ordinary duty to register and investigate a cognizable offence when one is disclosed.</p>

<h2>The four routes compared</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Route 1 — The police station.</strong> Approaching the police can produce an FIR under Section 85 BNS (cruelty) or other penal provisions where the allegations are cognizable. In Delhi, matrimonial cruelty complaints are ordinarily routed first through the Crime Against Women Cells, where conciliation is attempted before registration. The criminal route carries investigative powers — and consequences for the respondent — that the DV Act does not, but it offers the woman no residence or maintenance order.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Route 2 — The Protection Officer.</strong> Every district has Protection Officers appointed under Section 8. The PO records a Domestic Incident Report, forwards it to the Magistrate, assists the woman in drafting and filing a Section 12 application, and connects her to shelter homes, medical facilities and legal aid. The PO is the Act&rsquo;s own gateway — free, structured, and designed for a woman navigating the system without counsel.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Route 3 — A registered service provider.</strong> Voluntary organisations registered under Section 10 may record a DIR, arrange a medical examination, and secure shelter. For a woman not ready for either the police or the court, the service provider is a lawful intermediate step that still creates a contemporaneous official record.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Route 4 — The Magistrate directly.</strong> A Section 12 application may be presented straight to the Magistrate, usually through counsel, without any DIR preceding it. The Act requires the Magistrate to consider any DIR that has been received, but does not make one a condition of entertaining the application. This is the fastest road to interim orders under Section 23 where urgency — threatened dispossession, withheld finances — demands it.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the choice actually decides</h2>
<p>The routes are not mutually exclusive, and Section 36 confirms that the Act&rsquo;s remedies are in addition to, not in derogation of, any other law. But sequence matters. A woman whose immediate problems are a roof, money and safety is usually better served beginning with the civil machinery — the Section 12 application and interim reliefs — because those orders address her situation directly and quickly. A criminal complaint serves a different function: investigation and penal accountability. Filing both at once is lawful and common, but each filing becomes part of the record in the other, and in the matrimonial litigation that often follows. Exaggeration in one proceeding is cross-examination material in all of them. The sound practice is to report accurately, contemporaneously, and through the door that matches the need.</p>

<h2>Preserving the record at the reporting stage</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Medical examination first</strong> where there is physical injury — an MLC made the same day outweighs a month of later affidavits.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep the electronic trail:</strong> messages, call records, photographs of injuries or of locked doors, preserved in original form rather than screenshots alone.</li>
  <li><strong>Note the DIR contents before signing.</strong> The report is read by the Magistrate; an incomplete or inaccurate DIR follows the case.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask for the Section 5 information in writing</strong> — legal aid, shelter and the PO&rsquo;s details — so that nothing depends on memory of a difficult day.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The respondent&rsquo;s perspective</h2>
<p>For the man or family who learns that an incident has been reported, the reporting stage is equally consequential. A DIR is not a finding, an FIR is not a conviction, and a Section 12 notice is not a summons to a criminal trial — responding to each requires understanding what it is. The common error is to treat a DV application as a criminal case and respond with denial of everything, including the admitted facts of the relationship and household; the credibility lost there is rarely recovered. Measured, accurate responses at the first stage — through counsel, on the record — are worth more than aggression at any later one.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> The reporting route is the one decision in a DV matter made entirely without a judge. A woman in immediate danger should use the police; a woman whose need is shelter, maintenance and protection orders should reach the Magistrate — directly or through the Protection Officer — without waiting for the criminal process to mature. The Act was written precisely so that she does not have to choose one forever.</p>
</div>
